==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Flag of Prussia 1892-1918.svg|thumb|right|The flag of Prussia from 1892 to 1918.]]

===Etymology===
From the New {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|Prussia|lang=la}}<!--not 'Borussia'-->, a Latinization used by Peter of Dusburg of a {{etyl|bat|en}} ({{etyl|prg|en}}, or perhaps {{etyl|lt|-}} or {{etyl|lv|-}}) autonym. The {{etyl|enm|-}} designation for the region, {{term|Pruce|lang=enm}}, derives from the same Latinization and is the source of the terms {{term|pruce|lang=en}} and {{term|spruce|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɹʌʃə/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʌʃə|lang=en}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{context|chiefly|historical|lang=en}} A geographical area on the Baltic coast of northeastern Europe.
# {{context|historical|lang=en}} A Baltic country located in this area, conquered by the [[w:Teutonic Order|Teutonic Order]] and ultimately absorbed into Germany.
# {{context|historical|lang=en}} A German province which was originally located in this area but later greatly expanded, and which was the predecessor to and a member of the [[w:German Empire|German Empire]]; abolished as an administrative unit at the end of the Second World War.

====Usage notes====
In the Baltic languages the 'u' is long; it was also long in [[W:Middle English|Middle English]], but it has become short in modern English.

